# DiskBuffer — a concrete screenshot_buffer adapter : the circular on-disk frame
# writer. Declares the family it implements (the inverted arrow) ; the family
# never names it. Named for its IDENTITY (an on-disk circular buffer), NOT its
# transport — that it base64-decodes and writes JPEG bytes is a detail of HOW it
# buffers, configured in .world (dir + capacity).
#
# OUT-OF-PROCESS by construction : the effect runs in the generic adapter-host
# (`storehouse host`), never in the kernel — the kernel carries no adapters or
# families. The host resolves this adapter to its `handler` program, execs it
# with the FrameCaptured payload on stdin (frame_id, captured_at, frame_data),
# folds the .world block into the child env (BUFFER_DIR, BUFFER_CAPACITY), and
# maps the handler's exit code to the bind's verdict : exit 0 -> success_command
# (Screenshot.RecordStored), threading the stdout `path=...` line in. Because it
# is a SEPARATE PROCESS, a slow or wedged write can NEVER block the synchronous
# domain core — the whole point of an effect port.
#
# The bind `WebDebug::Screenshot.buffered_by("DiskBuffer", on: "FrameCaptured")`
# resolves IFF DiskBuffer's declared family (screenshot_buffer) carries the
# `buffered_by` verb — the typed attach checkpoint.
Hecks.adapter "DiskBuffer" do
  port    "screenshot_buffer"
end
